    <h2>Form tabs</h2>
    <h3>Form tab "Common parameters"</h3>
    <p>On this tab, you can configure the settings required to correctly include and load the Fancybox library on the page.</p>
    <ul class="g-help-list">
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_combo"></span> <b>Load libraries via.</b><br> This parameter allows you to choose the source from which Fancybox files and their required dependencies (such as jQuery) are loaded. Options include popular CDN networks (Yandex, Google, Cloudflare) or local files hosted on your own server. Using a CDN can often speed up site loading, thanks to a distributed network of servers and the possibility that the files are already stored in the user's browser cache. Loading files locally is useful if your project operates within a closed network without internet access, or if you need full control over versioning and wish to avoid dependency on external resources. Please note: the "jQuery CDN" option applies only to the jQuery library—which is required for older versions of Fancybox (2/3) but is not needed for Fancybox 4. Choose a source based on your project's specific requirements for speed, security, and availability.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Load jQuery library.</b><br> This checkbox enables or disables the automatic loading of the jQuery library, which is necessary for Fancybox to function. It is only required when using older versions of Fancybox (2 and 3) that depend on jQuery; without it, the plugin will fail to initialize, and errors will appear in the console. For the modern version, Fancybox 4, this setting is not needed—it is entirely independent of jQuery and runs on pure JavaScript. If you are unsure which version of Fancybox your project uses, it is best to leave this option disabled: it will have no effect on Fancybox 4, and it can always be enabled later if you are using an older version.</li> Avoid including jQuery "just in case"—an extra library increases page size and slows down loading times.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Include the jQuery Easing animation library.</b><br> This option enables or disables the loading of the jQuery Easing library, which adds extra easing types for Fancybox animations. It provides advanced motion options (such as "easeOutBack", "easeInElastic", etc.) that make transitions appear more expressive and natural. This library is only required when using jQuery in combination with older versions of Fancybox (2/3); it will not function without jQuery. This option is not needed for Fancybox 4, which utilizes native CSS transitions and modern easing functions, requiring no jQuery at all. Do not enable this option unless necessary, as additional scripts increase page size and can slow down loading. If you are unsure whether you need this library, it is safer to leave the option disabled.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Include the jQuery MouseWheel library.</b><br> This option enables or disables the loading of the jQuery MouseWheel library, which handles mouse wheel scrolling within a Fancybox modal window. It allows users to smoothly navigate through gallery slides or adjust the zoom level of a large image, rather than scrolling the entire webpage. This library is only required when using jQuery in combination with older versions of Fancybox (2/3); it will not function without jQuery. This option is not needed for Fancybox 4, which implements scrolling using pure JavaScript and does not depend on jQuery. Do not enable the flag unless absolutely necessary: ​​an extra script increases page weight and may conflict with other scroll handlers. If you are unsure whether this library is needed, it is safer to leave the flag disabled.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Form tab "Fancybox parameters"</h3>
    <p>This tab is used to configure the main parameters of the Fancybox widget. Settings are adjusted via a set of fields, each of which controls a specific aspect of the modal window's behavior and appearance—such as animations, navigation, buttons, content types, and more.</p>
    <ul class="g-help-list">
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_spinner"></span> <b>Space between the Fancybox wrapper and the content (px)</b><br> Sets the internal padding between the border of the Fancybox wrapper and its content. The value is specified in pixels (px). This allows you to adjust the empty space around the content within the modal window.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_spinner"></span> <b>Space between the visible area and the Fancybox (px)</b><br> Sets the distance between the edge of the visible screen area and the Fancybox window. The value is specified in pixels (px). This helps control how close the modal window is positioned to the edge of the screen.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Change content opacity for 'elastic' transitions</b><br> This option enables or disables changes to content opacity during an 'elastic' transition animation. When enabled, the opacity smoothly transitions from 0 to 1 (or vice versa), making the appearance and disappearance of images feel softer. If disabled, the opacity remains constant throughout the animation.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Display close button</b><br> This option enables or disables the display of a close button (an 'X' icon) within the Fancybox modal window. When enabled, users can manually close the window.</li> If this option is disabled, the window can only be closed via other methods (for example, by clicking outside the window or programmatically).</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Use the Esc key to close Fancybox.</b><br> This option enables or disables the ability to close a Fancybox modal window by pressing the Esc key. When enabled, users can quickly close the window using their keyboard, which helps improve the user experience (UX). If this option is disabled, the window can only be closed through other means (such as using the close button or clicking outside the window area).</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Enable gallery looping with "Next" / "Previous" buttons.</b><br> This option enables a looping mode for the Fancybox gallery and automatically displays "Next" and "Previous" navigation buttons. When enabled, users can navigate from the last slide back to the first, and from the first slide to the last, ensuring a continuous viewing experience. If this option is disabled, navigation is restricted to the boundaries of the gallery: the buttons will not appear, and it will not be possible to move beyond the start or end of the list.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Scale image to fit the viewport.</b><br> This option enables or disables automatic scaling of the image so that it fits entirely within the visible area of ​​the screen (the viewport). When enabled, the image adjusts to the size of the browser window—it will not exceed the boundaries and will not require scrolling to be viewed in its entirety. If disabled, the image is displayed at its original size, which may necessitate zooming or scrolling.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Center Fancybox when scrolling the page.</b><br> This option enables or disables the automatic centering of the Fancybox window within the visible area of ​​the screen as the page is scrolled. When enabled, the modal window remains perfectly centered even when the scroll position changes, which improves the user experience and ease of interaction. If disabled, the window’s position remains fixed relative to its original location on the page and may shift away from the center when scrolling.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Close Fancybox when clicking on the overlay.</b><br> This option enables or disables closing the Fancybox modal window by clicking on the darkened overlay (background) surrounding it. When enabled, users can quickly close the window by clicking anywhere outside its content—a standard and convenient interaction pattern. If this flag is disabled, the window can only be closed using other methods: via the close button, the Esc key, or programmatically.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Close Fancybox when clicking on the content.</b><br> This flag enables or disables closing the Fancybox modal window by clicking directly on its content (for example, on an image, video, or HTML block). When enabled, a single click on the content closes the window—a convenient feature for simple viewing scenarios. If this flag is disabled, clicking on the content does not close the window; it can only be closed via other methods (the close button, the Esc key, or by clicking on the overlay, provided those options are enabled).</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Automatically resize image upon loading.</b><br> This flag enables or disables the automatic adjustment of an image's size immediately after it is loaded into Fancybox. When enabled, the plugin calculates an optimal scale so that the entire image fits within the window without exceeding its boundaries or requiring scrolling. If this flag is disabled, the image is displayed at its original dimensions—which may result in it being too large or too small, or, in some cases, extending beyond the limits of the modal window.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_spinner"></span> <b>Resize duration when switching gallery items (ms).</b><br> This parameter sets the duration of the animation for resizing the Fancybox window when switching between items in a gallery. The value is specified in milliseconds (ms). A higher number results in a smoother, slower adjustment to the new item's size, while a lower number makes the transition sharper and faster. This helps prevent the interface from "jerking" if the gallery items differ significantly in their proportions or dimensions. Setting this to 0 disables the animation, causing the size to change instantly.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_spinner"></span> <b>Content fade-out duration when switching gallery items (ms).</b><br> This parameter defines the duration of the fade-out animation for the current content when transitioning to the next item in the Fancybox gallery. The value is specified in milliseconds (ms). A higher number results in a smoother, longer fade-out for the existing image or block, while a lower number makes the transition sharper. This helps make transitions between items of varying formats or types appear neater and more visually pleasing. If set to 0, the content will disappear instantly, without any smooth fade-out effect.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Set of fields "Easing used for elastic animations"</h4>
    <p>The following effects are applied when an image appears or disappears:<br>
    <b>swing</b> — adds a sense of "liveliness" to the motion, featuring a gentle start and finish with a noticeable acceleration in the middle.<br>
    <b>easeInQuad</b> — the element begins slowly, then smoothly and steadily gains speed following a quadratic curve.<br>
    <b>easeOutQuad</b> — the motion starts at a good speed and then slows down—gently yet perceptibly—following a quadratic pattern.<br>
    <b>easeInOutQuad</b> — provides a balanced transition: a smooth acceleration at the beginning and an equally smooth deceleration at the end.<br>
    <b>easeInCubic</b> — the start is almost imperceptible, followed by a more active increase in speed based on a cubic curve.<br>
    <b>easeOutCubic</b> — the element starts quickly and then noticeably "brakes" toward the end, creating a sense of a substantial conclusion.<br>
    <b>easeInOutCubic</b> — offers a balanced motion with a distinct acceleration and deceleration phase, following a cubic curve.<br>
    <b>easeInQuart</b> — the start is extremely slow—nearly static—followed by a sharp and energetic surge in speed.<br>
    <b>easeOutQuart</b> — the start is confident and fast, while the finish is exceptionally smooth, as if the element "comes to a rest" in place.<br>
    <b>easeInOutQuart</b> — a symmetrical curve with a very gentle beginning and an equally delicate conclusion.<br>
    <b>easeInQuint</b> — the start is virtually motionless, followed by a very sharp and powerful acceleration based on a fifth-degree curve.<br>
    <b>easeOutQuint</b> — the start is rapid, while the end is extremely smooth—as if the element "dissolves" into its destination point.<br>
    <b>easeInOutQuint</b> — extremely ...soft start and finish, with sharp, dynamic movement in the middle.<br>
    <b>easeInSine</b> — the movement begins almost imperceptibly and smoothly, as if "flowing" out of stillness, following a sinusoidal curve.<br>
    <b>easeOutSine</b> — starts with noticeable speed, but concludes in a very soft, "airy" way, as if the element is gently settling into place.<br>
    <b>easeInOutSine</b> — a delicate, symmetrical curve: both the start and the end are equally smooth, with no abrupt transitions.<br>
    <b>easeInExpo</b> — the initial frames are nearly static, followed by an exponential surge in speed, creating a "take-off" effect.<br>
    <b>easeOutExpo</b> — a rapid start followed by a precise yet soft deceleration; the movement appears controlled and confident.<br>
    <b>easeInOutExpo</b> — combines a sharp start with a similarly precise yet smooth stop, emphasizing the dynamic nature of the transition.<br>
    <b>easeInCirc</b> — the element seems to "roll" forward along an arc, starting slowly and gradually accelerating.<br>
    <b>easeOutCirc</b> — a quick start followed by a smooth "settling" into position, tracing the path of a quarter-circle.<br>
    <b>easeInOutCirc</b> — a rounded, symmetrical curve that creates a sense of soft, fluid motion, free from any sharp angles.<br>
    <b>easeInElastic</b> — before the main movement, the element pulls back slightly, then springs forward with a series of decaying oscillations.<br>
    <b>easeOutElastic</b> — upon reaching its target, the element "bounces" around it a few times before gradually coming to a rest.<br>
    <b>easeInOutElastic</b> — combines an initial "pull-back" with final springy oscillations, resulting in a highly... dynamic effect.<br>
    <b>easeInBack</b> — before starting, the element pulls back slightly, then surges forward, emphasizing the beginning of the movement.<br>
    <b>easeOutBack</b> — the element overshoots the target point and then returns, creating a subtle "overshoot" effect.<br>
    <b>easeInOutBack</b> — combines an initial pull-back with a final overshoot and return, making the movement particularly expressive.<br>
    <b>easeInBounce</b> — at the start, the element performs a series of diminishing "bounces" before beginning its main movement.<br>
    <b>easeOutBounce</b> — upon reaching the target, the element bounces several times—with each subsequent bounce being lower than the last—until it comes to a stop.<br>
    <b>easeInOutBounce</b> — combines initial bounces with final rebounds, creating a highly "bouncy" and playful effect.</p>
    <ul class="g-help-list">
