    <h4>Set of fields "Transition effect between images"</h4>
    <ul class="g-help-list">
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_combo"></span> <b>Transition effect for image appearance.</b><br> This parameter selects the animation type for when an image appears in Fancybox. The "fade" option makes the image visible through a smooth transition (fading in).<br>The "zoom" option typically creates an effect where the image expands from a small point or from the center.<br>The "none" option disables animation, causing the image to appear instantly.<br> Your choice affects the perceived speed and emphasis: "fade" offers a calm, subtle look; "zoom" is more noticeable; and "none" provides the fastest, most neutral experience.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_combo"></span> <b>Transition effect for image disappearance.</b><br> This parameter defines the animation type used when an image disappears upon closing the Fancybox window.<br>The "fade" option smoothly reduces the image's opacity until it vanishes completely.<br> The "zoom" option typically acts as a reverse zoom—the image shrinks toward the center or a single point before disappearing. The "none" option disables animation, causing the content to vanish instantly.<br> This choice influences the perceived fluidity of the interface: "fade" appears soft and calm, "zoom" adds a dynamic touch, and "none" ensures maximum speed and a minimalist feel.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_spinner"></span> <b>Animation speed for image appearance (ms).</b><br> This parameter sets the duration of the animation when an image appears in Fancybox—specifying how many milliseconds the effect (fade, zoom, etc.) will take. A higher value results in a slower, smoother appearance, while a lower value makes the image appear more quickly and abruptly.<br> For example, 300 ms provides a moderately smooth transition, 100 ms is quick and concise, and 0 ms results in an instant appearance with no animation. Choose a value based on the selected transition type and the overall rhythm of the interface: longer durations work well for more relaxed, understated galleries, while shorter durations are better for dynamic, fast-paced scenarios.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_spinner"></span> <b>Effect speed for hiding an image (ms).</b><br> This parameter defines the duration of the animation used when an image disappears upon closing the Fancybox window; the value is specified in milliseconds. A higher number results in a longer, smoother fade-out or shrinking effect, while a lower number makes the image disappear more abruptly. For example, 300 ms creates a gentle, noticeable fade-out; 100 ms is quick and concise; and 0 ms results in an instant disappearance with no animation. Select a value based on the transition type (fade, zoom, etc.) and the overall pace of the interface: a slightly longer duration is appropriate for more subtle, refined galleries, while a shorter duration suits faster-paced scenarios.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Set of fields "Overlay"</h4>
    <ul class="g-help-list">
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Show overlay.</b><br> This flag enables or disables the display of a darkened background (overlay) behind the Fancybox window.<br> When enabled, a semi-transparent layer appears over the page; it visually separates the modal window from the rest of the content and signals to the user that the primary interaction is currently focused on the image. Additionally, clicking on this layer often closes the window.<br> If the flag is disabled, the background is not shown: the window appears directly on the page, and it can only be closed via the "X" button, the Esc key, or other configured methods. Disabling the overlay can be useful for minimalist interfaces or in cases where it is important to keep the rest of the page content visible.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_spinner"></span> <b>Overlay opacity.</b><br> This parameter sets the opacity level for the darkened background (overlay) behind the Fancybox window.<br> The value is specified on a scale from 0 to 1, where 0 represents a completely transparent background (invisible), and 1 represents maximum opacity (nearly black).<br> For example, 0.7 is a classic choice: the background is noticeably darkened, yet page details remain discernible;<br> 0.3 provides a subtle darkening, which only slightly separates the window from the rest of the content. Choose a value based on your website's contrast and the level of emphasis you want to place on the image: use a higher value for vibrant, saturated pages, and a lower one for minimalist, light-themed designs.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_text"></span> <b>Overlay color.</b><br> This parameter defines the color of the darkened background (overlay) that appears behind the Fancybox window.<br>
    Specify it using a HEX code (e.g., #000000, #1a1a1a).</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Set of fields "Fancybox visible area dimensions"</h4>
    <ul class="g-help-list">
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_spinner"></span> <b>Width (px)</b><br> This parameter sets a fixed width for the visible area of the Fancybox window, in pixels.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_spinner"></span> <b>Height (px)</b><br> This parameter sets a fixed height for the visible area of the Fancybox window, in pixels.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Set of fields "Image title"</h4>
    <ul class="g-help-list">
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Show title.</b><br> This checkbox enables or disables the display of a title (caption) for an image within the Fancybox window. When enabled, the title appears either above or below the image, depending on your layout settings.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_combo"></span> <b>Title position.</b><br> This setting determines where the title (caption) appears in relation to the image within the Fancybox window. The "outside the image" option places the title above or below the image; this is the most straightforward and reliable approach, as the text does not obstruct the photo, and the block automatically adjusts its width on mobile devices. The "inside the image" option overlays the title onto the photo (typically at the top or bottom). While this saves space, you must ensure the text remains legible against the background—adding a semi-transparent backdrop or "underlay" is a common solution. The "before the image" option generally places the title ahead of the content in the document flow (for example, above the entire viewing area), which is useful if you want to separate the description from the media itself. Choose a position based on the length of your text and the level of contrast: "outside" is better for longer captions, while "inside" works well for concise labels.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Form tab "Version"</h3>
    <p>This tab contains key legal and technical information regarding the Fancybox widget. It displays the current version of the library, which helps in identifying available features and fixes, as well as avoiding compatibility issues with other scripts. The license type is also specified—this determines whether the widget can be used in commercial projects, whether the code may be modified or redistributed, and provides access to the license agreement.</p>
